.TH TMUX-WORKSPACE-RESUME 1 "October 2026" "tmux-workspace-resume 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tmux-workspace-resume \- save and restore a tmux/byobu workspace across reboots
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tmux-workspace-resume
.RI [ \-q ]
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B byobu-tmux-workspace-resume
.RI [ \-q ]
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tmux-workspace-resume
snapshots every tmux session, window, pane, working directory and layout of the running
server and rebuilds them on a fresh server, typically the one byobu starts after a reboot.
For each pane it asks a chain of
.I resumers
(see
.BR tmux-workspace-resume-resumers (7))
whether the program running there can be relaunched with its state: Claude Code conversations
come back with
.B claude --resume
.IR session-id ,
ssh connections with their original command line, and any program listed in
.B RESUME_COMMANDS
with its arguments.
.PP
Snapshots are written to
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-workspace-resume/snapshots/ ,
the newest one being
.IR last.json .
Saving happens on tmux events (new window, split, rename, layout change, ...) through hooks,
periodically through a systemd user timer, on
.B prefix + C-s
and, most reliably, through
.B tmux-workspace-resume reboot
and
.BR "tmux-workspace-resume shutdown" .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR save " [" \-\-debounce "] [" \-\-force "] [" \-\-timer ]
Write a snapshot of the running server.
.B \-\-debounce
waits
.B SAVE_DEBOUNCE_S
seconds and coalesces a burst of events (what the hooks use).
.B \-\-timer
marks the periodic save of the systemd timer: with
.BR TIMER_SAVE=after\-input ,
it is skipped unless an attached client had input since the newest snapshot.
Without
.BR \-\-force ,
nothing is written while a restore is in progress, a snapshot from a previous boot is kept
until a restore has run on the current server (so a freshly started server cannot overwrite
the workspace that is about to be brought back), and an unchanged workspace only refreshes the
newest snapshot's modification time instead of writing an identical copy.
.TP
.BR restore " [" \-\-dry\-run "] [" \-\-layout\-only "] [" \-\-auto "] [" \fIsnapshot\fR ]
Rebuild the sessions of
.I snapshot
(default:
.IR last )
on the running server. Windows that already exist are skipped, so restoring twice is harmless.
When the saved session does not exist and the server has a single session, that session is
reused and renamed so the attached client follows; its single window is removed at the end
if only an idle shell is in it, otherwise it is kept after the last restored window.
.B \-\-dry\-run
prints the plan and touches nothing;
.B \-\-layout\-only
rebuilds windows and panes without relaunching programs;
.B \-\-auto
is what the plugin runs at server start: it obeys
.BR AUTO_RESTORE ,
runs at most once per server and stays silent.
.TP
.B list
Snapshots on disk, newest first, with their boot id and a summary.
.TP
.B status
Where tmux-workspace-resume is enabled, whether the hooks and the timer are active, the newest snapshot.
.TP
.BR enable " [" \-\-byobu | \-\-tmux ]
Wire the plugin into the user's byobu configuration
.RI ( $BYOBU_CONFIG_DIR/.tmux.conf ,
sourced last by byobu) or into
.IR ~/.tmux.conf ,
install the hooks on a running server and enable the systemd user timer. The default flavour
is byobu when byobu is installed or the command was invoked as
.BR byobu-tmux-workspace-resume .
The configuration change is a marker-delimited block that
.B disable
removes again.
.TP
.B disable
Undo
.BR enable .
.TP
.BR resumers " [" list "|" probe " \fIpane-id\fR]"
List the effective resumers, or show which one claims a live pane and what it would run.
.TP
.BR shutdown ", " reboot
Save with
.B \-\-force
and then run
.B systemctl poweroff
or
.BR "systemctl reboot" .
This is the reliable way to power off: the shutdown hook installed by
.B enable
runs when the user session ends, but systemd does not guarantee that the tmux server is still
alive at that moment.
.TP
.BR version ", " help
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print nothing on stderr; the log in
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-workspace-resume/tmux-workspace-resume.log
is always written.
.SH HOW A REBOOT WORKS
.IP 1. 4
.B tmux-workspace-resume enable
once. From then on every layout change and every 5 minutes produce a snapshot.
.IP 2. 4
.BR "tmux-workspace-resume reboot" ,
or any other reboot: the newest snapshot is at most a few minutes old.
.IP 3. 4
Log in and start
.BR byobu .
Its configuration runs
.IR tmux-workspace-resume.tmux ,
which installs the hooks and starts
.B tmux-workspace-resume restore \-\-auto
in the background. The snapshot is from a previous boot, so the windows, names, layouts and
directories come back within a second, and each pane's program is typed into its shell once the
prompt is up: the Claude panes resume their exact conversations, ssh panes reconnect.
.IP 4. 4
A second
.B byobu
in another terminal attaches through byobu's usual grouped session; nothing is restored twice.
.SH TMUX WITHOUT BYOBU
Add
.B set -g @plugin 'bumml-foss/tmux-workspace-resume'
to
.I ~/.tmux.conf
and install it with TPM, or run
.BR "tmux-workspace-resume enable \-\-tmux" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tmux-workspace-resume/config
Settings, see
.IR /usr/share/doc/tmux-workspace-resume/examples/config .
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tmux-workspace-resume/resumers/
User resumers; a file with the same name as a shipped one replaces it.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-workspace-resume/snapshots/
Snapshots,
.IR last.json ,
and with
.B SCROLLBACK_LINES
a
.I <snapshot>.scrollback/
directory of the panes' history beside each snapshot.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/tmux-workspace-resume/
Per-boot markers and locks.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Every configuration key can be set as
.BI TWR_ KEY
and takes precedence over the file.
.B TWR_NO_RESTORE=1
disables the automatic restore for one server start.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux-workspace-resume-resumers (7),
.BR tmux (1),
.BR byobu (1)
